        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. The picks were made from published specifications,
            maker and retailer listings, warranty terms and independent coverage, all checked on 15
            October 2026. Two of them have their own pages on this site — our reviews of the
            Horizon 7.0 AT and the SOLE F63 — and those are readings of the specifications and owner
            reports, not hands-on tests either.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>On sale now:</strong> each machine had to be in stock
            on its maker's own store on 15 October 2026, under that name, at a price inside its band.
            <strong>Agreement:</strong> deck or belt size, speed range, incline and weight capacity had
            to match between the maker and at least one source that does not make the machine.
            <strong>A real warranty:</strong> written terms from a brand with its own store and support
            route. <strong>No safety action:</strong> we checked the US Consumer Product Safety
            Commission's recall database and warnings for every brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several machines are missing on purpose. The <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101</a>,
            long the default answer at about $700, showed as unavailable on Horizon's own store on 15
            October as the T101 Connect; one major retailer still listed a T101 at $698, but its listing
            did not let us confirm which version it was. You should also know that an earlier version,
            the T101-05, was recalled in October 2022 because it could speed up, slow down or stop
            unexpectedly — the remedy was a software repair — and in 2026 the CPSC announced a civil
            penalty against Horizon's importer for reporting that hazard late. The current Connect is
            a later model, but check the model and serial number on any discounted or used T101.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Sunny Health &amp; Fitness SF-T7603, a common under-$500 folding treadmill, was shown
            as sold out at $449.99 on Sunny's own store. The NordicTrack T Series 5, which has sold in
            the $420 to $600 range, is not listed on NordicTrack's US site, and we could not confirm a
            current price on a retailer page we could read on the day. And Sperax walking pads, which
            still turn up at budget prices on marketplaces, are under a CPSC warning telling owners to
            stop using them. We found no recall or warning covering the MERACH, Goplus, NordicTrack or SOLE models on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget-bands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Each Budget Band Buys</h2>
<p>
            This page gives you the one or two machines we would buy in each band. Each band also has
            a full guide on this site with more options, so treat the links below as the deeper read
            once you know which band you are in.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Around $150 to $300: a walking pad</h3>
          <p>
            At this price you are buying a motorised deck with a remote, usually no rails, a top speed
            of about 4 mph and a belt around 35 to 43 inches long. That is a walking machine, and for
            walking it can be genuinely good. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>
            covers five of them, including the cheapest pad we could verify.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Around $300 to $500: a compact folding machine</h3>
          <p>
            This is the awkward band. The money buys either a walking pad with a fold-up bar and a
            faster motor, or a small folding treadmill with rails but a short, narrow deck. A
            full-size folding treadmill with a 55-inch deck occasionally dips into it on sale, but on
            the day we checked none that met our rules was both in stock and under $500. The
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500 guide</a>
            looks at the formats in this band, including used and refurbished machines.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Around $600 to $1,000: a full-size jogging treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            This is where a budget treadmill becomes a treadmill in the gym sense: side rails, a
            powered incline, a console and a 55 or 60-inch deck. It is the band with the biggest jump
            in what you get for each extra dollar, and for most people who want to jog it is the
            sensible floor. See the guides to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $800</a>
            and the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $1,000</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Around $1,000 to $1,500: a running treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            The extra money buys steeper incline (15% rather than 10 or 12), faster incline motors,
            heavier frames and, most usefully, longer parts warranties. This is the cheapest band in
            which a machine is built around regular running rather than tolerating it. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmills-under-1500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills under $1,500</a>
            compares more of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Above $1,500 you are no longer shopping on a budget, and the trade-offs change from
            "what is missing" to "what is worth paying for". That is the territory of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$2,000 guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing-your-band" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Choosing Your Band in Four Questions</h2>
<p>
            Most people pick a budget first and a machine second. It works better the other way
            round: decide what the machine has to do, and the band follows. Four questions settle it
            for most buyers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Will you run, or only walk?</strong> If the honest answer is walking, even
            brisk walking on an incline, you are in the walking pad band and anything more is
            optional. If you want to jog a few times a week, the floor is a full-size deck, and the
            compact-folder band is a compromise at best. Our page on
            <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you can run on a walking pad</a>
            explains why the line falls where it does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. How tall are you, and how long is your stride?</strong> A belt of about 41 inches
            suits walkers of most heights at desk pace. Taller users, and anyone who wants to move
            beyond a walk, will feel the front and back of a short belt quickly. If you are over about
            six feet, give more weight to deck length than to any other figure on the card.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Where will it live?</strong> A walking pad slides under a sofa. A compact
            folder stands in a corner. A full-size folding treadmill still needs a floor area of
            roughly 6 by 3 feet in use, plus clear space behind it, and folds to a tall, heavy block
            rather than disappearing. Measure first, using our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions and space requirements</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. How many years do you want from it?</strong> If you want a machine to last
            through several years of regular running, the parts warranty points you to the top band.
            If you want to find out whether you will use a treadmill at all, a cheaper machine is a
            reasonable experiment — as long as it is the right kind of machine for what you plan to
            do on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are still unsure between a pad and a full treadmill after those four, our
            comparison of
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads and treadmills</a>
            sets the two side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-budget-sacrifices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What "Budget" Sacrifices: Motor, Deck and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            Every step down in price removes something. Screens and app features are the visible
            cuts, and the least important. Three less visible ones matter more, and they are worth
            understanding before you choose a band.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The motor's continuous rating</h3>
          <p>
            What keeps a treadmill running smoothly under a heavier runner, hour after hour, is the
            motor's continuous rating — what it can deliver all day without overheating — not the
            peak figure printed on the box. Budget machines are where the gap between the two is
            widest. MERACH is unusual in publishing both for the W50 TrekPad: 3.5 HP peak and 1.25
            HP continuous. That is fine for walking, which is what the pad is for, and it is why a
            pad motor would struggle with daily jogging. The Goplus is sold as 3.75 HP with no word
            on whether that is continuous, so read it as a peak figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Full-size budget machines are better, but the figure is harder to pin down. Horizon does
            not publish a motor rating on its product pages; reviewers quote one, but it is not the
            maker's number. SOLE quotes 3.0 HP for the F63. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the difference between the ratings, and why comparing headline figures across
            brands rarely tells you much.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck length</h3>
          <p>
            Deck length is the sacrifice you feel on day one. Walking needs a belt of roughly 45 to 50
            inches to be comfortable for most adults; jogging wants about 55; running wants 60. The
            walking pad and compact folder on this page have 41.3-inch and 52-inch belts. Every full-size
            machine here has a 60-inch deck, which is why the jump from $500 to $900 is the most
            important step on the page. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            sets out the lengths by height and pace.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Warranties are the most honest signal of how long a maker expects a machine to last.
            Walking pads at this price are typically covered for about a year; MERACH gives two years
            on the W50 TrekPad. The Horizon and SOLE machines
            here all carry lifetime frame and motor cover, which sounds equal — but the parts warranty
            is where they differ, and parts are what actually fail: one year on the Horizon T202, two
            years on the SOLE F63 and three years on the Horizon 7.0 AT. Check NordicTrack's current
            terms for the T Series 10, which we did not confirm for this page. Labour is one
            year on most of them. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read these terms and what "lifetime" usually means in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-cheap-gets-expensive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Where Cheap Gets Expensive</h2>
<p>
            The purchase price is only the first cost of a treadmill. A cheap machine can end up
            costing more than a slightly dearer one in four predictable ways, and our comparison of
            <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap and premium treadmills</a>
            goes through the long-run arithmetic in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buying twice.</strong> The most expensive budget treadmill is the one that turns
            out to be the wrong kind. A $300 walking pad bought by someone who wants to jog will be
            replaced within months, and the total spend is then the pad plus a real treadmill. Buy for
            the most demanding thing you will do every week, not the gentlest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Out-of-warranty parts.</strong> When a motor controller or console fails in year
            two, a one-year parts warranty means you pay for the part and, usually, the technician.
            On a budget machine one repair can be a large share of what the treadmill cost. Our guide
            to <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance and repair costs</a>
            gives typical ranges.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery and set-up.</strong> Full-size budget treadmills weigh roughly 190 to 280
            lb assembled and ship as freight, usually to the curb. Makers' own stores often list
            standard delivery as a separate charge from the treadmill, with room-of-choice delivery
            and assembly costing more. Add those charges before comparing
            prices, and check whether the machine will fit up your stairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running it near its limits.</strong> A machine rated close to your weight, used
            every day at the top of its speed range, wears faster than one with headroom. Leaving a
            margin of 50 lb or more under the weight capacity, and keeping to the maker's lubrication
            schedule, are the two cheapest ways to protect a budget treadmill. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-make-a-cheap-treadmill-last-longer/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making a cheap treadmill last longer</a>
            covers the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this means you should spend more than you can afford. It means the right budget
            is the one that buys a machine suited to your use with a warranty that covers the years
            you expect to use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-budget-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How to Tell a Real Budget Price From a Fake One</h2>
<p>
            Budget treadmills are sold almost permanently "on sale", which makes it hard to know what
            a good price is. A few habits help.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ignore the compare-at price.</strong> On 15 October 2026 the Horizon 7.0 AT was
            listed at $1,099 against a compare-at price of $2,100, and on 5 November MERACH listed the
            W50 TrekPad at $299.99 against $479.99. The second number is rarely what anyone pays. The price that matters is
            the one a machine has actually sold at more than once.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Expect discounts to come back.</strong> Horizon had the T202 at $899 against a
            $1,299 compare-at price, and machines in this band are discounted repeatedly through the
            year. If a machine
            you want is at its regular price, waiting a few weeks is often worth it. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
            explains the usual cycle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Compare the maker's store with retailers.</strong> Prices for the same machine
            differ between sellers on the same day, and promotional prices are often limited by region
            or to the maker's own store, so compare the maker's price with Amazon and a major
            retailer before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check what the price includes.</strong> Delivery, assembly, a heart rate strap or
            an app trial can make a cheaper listing the more expensive purchase. If you are buying on
            Amazon, confirm who is selling and shipping the machine, since that decides who honours
            the return and the warranty; our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            covers the checks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Used and Refurbished: The Other Budget Route</h2>
<p>
            If the band you can afford does not buy the machine you need, a second-hand or
            manufacturer-refurbished treadmill can move you up a band for the same money. A used
            full-size treadmill that cost $1,500 new can sell for a fraction of that, and a solid frame
            often outlasts its first owner's interest in running. Our guide to
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-cheap-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a cheap treadmill</a>
            ranks those routes, and clearance and open-box, by what they really cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            The risks are real and specific: no warranty, or a short one; an unknown history of
            lubrication and belt wear; a motor controller that may be near the end of its life; and,
            occasionally, a model that has been recalled. The T101-05 recall described above is a
            good example of why the model and serial number on a used machine are worth checking
            against the CPSC database before you hand over money. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a>
            takes you through an inspection, and
            <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills</a>
            covers machines that come back with a limited warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: a used machine is a good budget route for a full-size running
            treadmill, where the frame is the expensive part and lasts. It is a poor route for walking
            pads, where the motor and controller are most of the value and a used one has spent part
            of its short life already.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-spend-differently" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Spend More, and Who Should Spend Less</h2>
<p>
            The bands above suit most buyers. Some should deliberately move up or down one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Spend less if you will only walk.</strong> If you are buying for desk walking or
            gentle daily steps, a full-size treadmill is money spent on deck length and speed you will
            not use. A well-chosen walking pad is the better buy, and our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            explains what separates a good one from a frustrating one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Spend more if you will run most days.</strong> Daily running is the hardest work a
            treadmill does, and the parts warranty matters more than the screen. If you are in that
            group, read the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a>
            before settling on the bottom of the running band.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Spend more if you are near a weight limit.</strong> Every full-size machine on this
            page is rated to 325 lb, and the walking pads to 265 lb. If you are within about 50 lb of
            those figures, look at the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            instead of stretching a budget machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider no motor at all.</strong> A manual treadmill does steep walking for
            around $200 with no outlet and few electronics to fail, though it is not for everyone;
            our guide to the
            <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best manual treadmills</a>
            is honest about the downsides.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if the honest answer is that you will use a treadmill a few times a month, consider
            whether you need one yet. A cheap machine that becomes a clothes rail is the most
            expensive treadmill of all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best budget treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the budget. Around $300, the best buy is a walking pad with incline such as the MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad. Around $900, it is a full-size machine with a 60-inch deck, such as the Horizon T202, or the NordicTrack T Series 10 at about $1,000 if you want a touchscreen. Around $1,100, the Horizon 7.0 AT or SOLE F63 add 15% incline and longer parts cover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good cheap treadmill for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For regular running, look for a 60-inch deck, a 12 mph top speed and a parts warranty of more than one year. On the day we checked, the cheapest machines we could verify meeting those tests were the Horizon T202 at $899 and the NordicTrack T Series 10 at about $999. Walking pads and compact folders under $500 have belts of 41 to 52 inches, which is too short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on a budget treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spend enough to buy the right kind of machine for your most demanding weekly use. For walking, about $300 buys a good pad. For jogging and some running, about $900 is the realistic floor for a full-size deck. For running most days, about $1,100 buys steeper incline and longer parts cover. Spending less than your use needs usually means buying twice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap treadmills worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A cheap treadmill is worth it when it matches how you will use it. A $300 walking pad used for daily walking is excellent value. The same pad bought for running is not, because its belt is short and its motor is rated for walking. The mistakes come from buying a machine for a lighter job than the one you will actually give it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do you lose with a budget treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly three things: motor headroom, deck length and warranty. Budget motors are often rated far lower for continuous use than the headline figure suggests, cheaper decks are shorter, and parts warranties shrink to one or two years. Screens and app features are also cut, but those matter less to how long the machine lasts and how it feels to run on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to buy a used treadmill than a cheap new one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a full-size running treadmill, a good used machine can be better value, because the frame is the expensive part and tends to last. For walking pads, buy new, since the motor and controller are most of the value. Check any used machine against the CPSC recall database, inspect the belt and deck, and expect little or no warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Budget Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a sensible saving into a more expensive purchase. All of them are avoidable before you order.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for your gentlest use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">People buy for the walk they do most days and then want to jog. A walking pad or a 42-inch compact folder will not cope with regular jogging, and replacing it within the year costs more than buying the right machine first. Buy for the most demanding session you expect each week, and you will rarely need to buy again.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing headline horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Budget motors are sold on peak figures that can be several times their continuous rating, and some makers do not publish a rating at all. Comparing those numbers across brands tells you very little. Compare deck length, top speed, incline range, weight capacity and the parts warranty instead; together they say far more about how hard a machine is built to work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading only the frame and motor warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lifetime frame and motor cover is common even on budget full-size machines, so it does not separate them. What does is the parts warranty — one year on some machines, three on others — because parts such as the motor controller and console fail first. Read the full breakdown, including labour, before deciding two machines are equivalent.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting delivery and placement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full-size budget treadmill weighs roughly 190 to 280 lb assembled and usually arrives at the curb. Delivery upgrades, a mat, a suitable outlet and the space behind the deck all add cost or effort. Check the price including delivery, the route into the room and the outlet type before you order, not when the freight truck arrives.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

